\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Can one compute the zeta function of $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z)$,
\[\zeta_n(s)=\prod_{p\mid n}(1-p^{-s})^{-1},\]
without first factoring $n$? For a polynomial $f\in\mathbb F_q[x]$, consider the analogous product over its monic irreducible factors. From this zeta data, can one recover the factorization of $f$ in deterministic polynomial time?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://aimath.org/WWN/primesinp/primesinp.pdf}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can one compute the zeta function of $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z)$,
\[\zeta_n(s)=\prod_{p\mid n}(1-p^{-s})^{-1},\]
without first factoring $n$? For a polynomial $f\in\mathbb F_q[x]$, consider the analogous product over its monic irreducible factors. From this zeta data, can one recover the factorization of $f$ in deterministic polynomial time?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 20001259 (AIM-COMPUTATION-0097). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://aimath.org/WWN/primesinp/primesinp.pdf}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:20001259}

